d). We can form chows:  \n- If we draw W1: we have W1-W2-W3 chow (using W2, W3) and leftover W3, W4, T1 pair, T5-T6, B3 pair. Still need one more chow or pair.  \n- If we draw W5: we have W3-W4-W5 chow, similar.  \n- If we draw T4: we have T4-T5-T6 chow.  \n- If we draw T7: we have T5-T6-T7 chow.  \n- If we draw B2: we have B2-B3-B4 chow? But we discarded B4, so B2-B3-B4 not possible unless we have B4, but we discarded it. B2 with B3 pair doesn't form chow.  \n- If we draw B5: we have B3-B4-B5? No B4.  \n\nSo discarding B4 might reduce flexibility with B suits. However, we already have an exposed Chow in B, so focusing on W and T suits might be better.  \n\nAlternatively, discarding **T5** might be good because T5 is middle tile, but we have T6, so T5-T6 can become chow with T4 or T7. Keeping T5 might be better.  \n\nConsidering the game state and opponents' melds:  \n- Previous player has Chow W1-W2-W3 and T4-T5-T6, so W1, W2, W3, T4, T5, T6 are less likely to be drawn.  \n- Across player has Chow B6-B7-B8 and Pung T9, so T9 is gone, B6-B8 are less likely.  \n- Next player has no melds.  \n\nThus, drawing tiles like W4, W5, T1, T7, B2, B3 might be more available.  \n\nGiven the need to maintain pair for winning, we have pairs in T1 and B3. We might need one pair to win, so keeping both pairs is good.  \n\nDiscarding **W4** might be best because it's a lone tile in W suit, and we have W2-W3, which can become chow with W1 or W4-W5 with W4 and W5, but without W5, W4 is less useful. Discarding W4 keeps the W2-W3 sequence for W1 or W5 draw, and retains all pairs.  \n\nAfter discarding W4, hand: W2, W3, W3, T1, T1, T5, T6, B3, B3, B4. We have 10 tiles, need one more to complete 14 with meld. Potential chows:  \n- Draw W1: chow W1-W2-W3 (uses W2, W3)  \n- Draw W5: chow W3-W4-W5? But we discarded W4, so need W4 and W5, not possible. So W5 alone not helpful for chow with W3.  \nThus, W4 discard might reduce W suit potential.  \n\nGiven the analysis, **B4** seems the safest discard as it's redundant and doesn't break pairs or key sequences.  \n\nReason: Discarding B4 preserves pairs in T1 and B3, maintains sequence potential in W and T suits, and removes a redundant tile from an already completed meld suit, improving hand flexibility without sacrificing scoring opportunities. The hand currently has low scoring potential, so we need to keep options open for drawing tiles that can form chows and possibly aim for patterns like All Chows, No Honors, and wait patterns to reach 8 points.  \n\nAnswer: Play B4"}
