Simples (2) + Self Drawn (1) + maybe Closed Wait (1) + maybe Last Tile (?) not sure. That's only 6. Need more.\\n- Mixed Straight (8) if I can get chows 123, 456, 789 in different suits. I have T4,T5,T6 (456 in Bamboo). I need 123 in one suit and 789 in another. My hand has W2,W3,W4, which could become 234 if I get W1? Not 123. Or could aim for 789 in Dots? I have B3, not 789. So difficult.\\n- Maybe Reversible Tiles (8 points)? Check reversible tiles list: B1,B2,B3,B4,B5,B8,B9, T2,T4,T5,T6,T8,T9, J3. My hand: W2,W3,W4,T1,B3. W tiles are not reversible except maybe W? No, W not listed. So not.\\n- Chicken Hand (8 points) if no other patterns, but Chicken Hand only counts if hand would be 0 fans. But if I have All Simples and All Chows, that's 4 points, so not Chicken.\\n\\nSo I need to aim for higher point patterns. Perhaps I should consider keeping terminals to try for Outside Hand (4 points) or Pung of Terminals (1 point each). But Outside Hand requires every meld and pair to have terminals or honors. My current melds [B3,B4,B5] and [T4,T5,T6] have no terminals/honors, so Outside Hand impossible unless I change melds? But melds are fixed once exposed. So Outside Hand not possible.\\n\\nWhat about Half Flush? My hand has multiple suits, but if I discard all but one suit, might be possible? But my melds include B and T suits, so Half Flush requires only one suit and honors. Since melds are of different suits, Half Flush impossible.\\n\\nAll Types (6 points) requires all three suits and winds and dragons. I have no winds or dragons in hand, but I could get one. My melds are B and T, so I have two suits. Need W suit and wind/dragon. Possible if I draw. But then my hand would need a wind/dragon meld or pair. \\n\\nGiven the complexity, maybe the best is to discard the least useful tile to improve hand shape towards a ready hand, and hope for high-point patterns later.\\n\\nI think T1 is the least useful because it's terminal and not connected to other tiles. B3 at least is simple and could pair. So discard T1.\\n\\nBut wait, I have two T1s. Discarding one T1 leaves one T1, which becomes a singleton. That might be worse because then I have a lone terminal. Perhaps discard both T1s over time? But now I can only discard one.\\n\\nMaybe discard B3 is better because B3 is also isolated, and keeping T1 pair might be good for a pair. But T1 pair is terminal pair, which could be the winning pair, but then I might not get All Simples. However, All Simples is only 2 points, maybe not crucial.\\n\\nConsider potential waits: If I keep W2,W3,W4 and have a pair of T1, then I need one more meld from the remaining W3 and B3s. That meld could be a pung of B3 if I draw a B3, or a chow involving B3 and something else. But that's uncertain.\\n\\nIf I keep W2,W3,W4 and pair of B3, then need meld from W3 and T1s. That could be pung of T1 if draw T1, or chow T1,T2,T3.\\n\\nWhich is more likely? Unshown T1: 3 left, B3: 3 left, W3: 3 left. Equal.\\n\\nBut T1 is terminal, so it might be harder to use in chows because it needs T2,T3 specifically. B3 can form chows with B1,B2 or B2,B4 or B3,B4,B5. B3,B4,B5 is already used in my meld, but could still form another? There are four B3s total, I have two in hand, one used in meld? Actually, the meld [B3,B4,B5] uses one B3. So there are B3s left. But forming chow B2,B3,B4 requires B2 and B4. Unshown B2:4 left, B4:2 left, so possible.\\n\\nGiven that, B3 might be more flexible because it can connect to both sides. T1 only connects to T2,T3 (T2:4 left, T3:3 left). So both have potential.\\n\\nBut I have two T1s, so keeping them as a pair is fine. If I discard one T1, I break the pair, leaving a singleton T1 which is bad. So if I want to keep a pair, I should keep either T1 pair or B3 pair. I have two of each. So I should not discard a tile from a potential pair unless I have another pair.\\n\\nI have three candidates for pair: W3 (two copies), T1 (two), B3 (two). So I can afford to discard one from a pair if I still have another pair. But if I discard one T1, I have one T1 left, so no T1 pair. Then my pairs are W3 and B3. But W3 is also a pair candidate. So I could discard one T1, then have pairs: W3 (two) and B3 (two). That works. Then my hand becomes: W2, W3(2), W4, T1(1), B3(2). That's 7 tiles after discard. Then I have pairs: W3 and B3. But I can only have one pair in the final hand. So I would need to use one as pair and the other as part of a meld. For example, use W3 as pair, then B3 pair needs to be broken to form a meld? Or use B3 as pair, then W3 pair needs to be broken.\\n\\nActually, in the final hand, you have exactly one pair. So having two potential pairs means I need to convert one into a meld. So it's okay to have multiple pairs now.\\n\\nSo if I discard T1, I lose a terminal but keep two simple pairs. That might be good for All Simples.\\n\\nIf I discard B3, I keep T1 pair and W3 pair. T1 pair is terminal, so breaks All Simples. So for All Simples, I should avoid keeping terminal pair.\\n\\nThus, to aim for All Simples, I should discard T1.\\n\\nAll Simples is 2 points, and if I also get All Chows (2 points), that's 4. Plus maybe Self Drawn (1), Closed Wait (1), etc., but still short of 8. However, I might get other patterns like Mixed Straight if I draw well. Or maybe Two Concealed Pungs (2 points) if I get pungs, but my hand is mostly chows.\\n\\nGiven the time, I'll choose to discard T1 to move towards All Simples and keep flexibility.\\n\\nSo my final choice: Play T1.\\n\\nBut let's confirm: valid actions include Play T1. Yes.\\n\\nReason: Discarding T1 removes a terminal, making All Simples possible, and keeps the connected W2-W3-W4 sequence and pairs of W3 and B3 for flexibility. T1 is less useful for chows due to being terminal and having fewer connecting tiles.\\n\\nEvaluation: Discarding T1 is the most promising move to improve hand shape and potential scoring patterns.\\n\\nAnswer: Play T1.\"}"}
